insert into public.script_contents (script_id, body) values ('5c1a0000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', '## [00:00] HOOK
(VISUAL: slow push-in on a coffee cup on a desk, steam rising)
Right now, while you sit perfectly still, you are moving at about sixteen hundred kilometres an hour. You can''t feel it, because everything around you is moving with you. The desk. The air. The coffee.
Now imagine the planet stops. Not slows down. Stops. For one single second.
The ground halts. You don''t.

## [00:35] SETUP
(VISUAL: globe with a glowing line along the equator, speed counter ticking)
Earth spins once every twenty-four hours. At the equator that means covering roughly forty thousand kilometres a day. Faster than a passenger jet. Faster than the speed of sound.

## [01:10] THE FIRST MILLISECOND
(VISUAL: slow-motion; a city street, everything lifting sideways)
At the equator, anything not anchored to bedrock keeps moving east at around 1,670 kilometres an hour. People, cars, trees, buildings. The atmosphere too.
Closer to the poles the speed drops, which is the only good news in this video.

## [02:30] THE WIND
(VISUAL: weather map, a wall of wind racing across continents)
The air above us doesn''t stop either. Supersonic winds would scour the surface, stronger than any hurricane ever recorded.

## [04:00] THE OCEANS
(VISUAL: ocean surface tilting, a wave taller than skyscrapers)
Oceans would surge eastward and pile onto coastlines. Then the planet starts spinning again, and everything slams the other way.

## [06:30] COULD IT HAPPEN?
(VISUAL: calm Earth from orbit)
Here''s the reassuring part. Nothing we know of could stop Earth in a second. Its rotation is slowing, but by milliseconds per century.

## [08:45] OUTRO
So the next time you sit still, remember: you''re riding a planet at supersonic speed. And that''s exactly how we like it.
(CTA: subscribe for the next "What If")') on conflict (script_id) do update set body = excluded.body;
