values ('5c1a0000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005', 'delhi-1739-nadir-shah', 'The Day Delhi Fell: Nadir Shah, 1739', 'How the Peacock Throne left India', 'history', 'English', 'YouTube Long-form', 14, 2010, 699, 6999, true, 'The Battle of Karnal, a rumour in Chandni Chowk, and a single day that emptied the Mughal treasury. A carefully sourced narrative with era-appropriate visual direction and a closing reflection on imperial decline.', '## [00:00] COLD OPEN
(VISUAL: candlelit marble hall, a jewelled throne in silhouette)
In the spring of 1739, a Persian king sat on the most famous throne in the world. It wasn''t his. Weeks earlier it had belonged to the Mughal emperor of India.
By the time he rode home, he would take it with him, along with a diamond whose name you already know.

## [00:40] KARNAL
(VISUAL: animated map, Persian army moving east through the Khyber)
Nadir Shah had risen from a shepherd''s family to the throne of Persia. In February 1739, at Karnal, about a hundred kilometres north of Delhi, his army met the forces of Emperor Muhammad Shah.', array['mughal', 'india', 'history', 'koh-i-noor']::text[], true, true, 156, 4.8, '2026-09-21T10:00:00.000Z')
