insert into public.script_contents (script_id, body) values ('5c1a0000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005', '## [00:00] COLD OPEN
(VISUAL: candlelit marble hall, a jewelled throne in silhouette)
In the spring of 1739, a Persian king sat on the most famous throne in the world. It wasn''t his. Weeks earlier it had belonged to the Mughal emperor of India.
By the time he rode home, he would take it with him, along with a diamond whose name you already know.

## [00:40] KARNAL
(VISUAL: animated map, Persian army moving east through the Khyber)
Nadir Shah had risen from a shepherd''s family to the throne of Persia. In February 1739, at Karnal, about a hundred kilometres north of Delhi, his army met the forces of Emperor Muhammad Shah.

## [02:00] THE BATTLE
(VISUAL: cavalry charge, smoke)
The battle at Karnal was over within hours. Persian musketeers and disciplined cavalry overwhelmed a larger but divided Mughal force.

## [04:00] ENTERING DELHI
(VISUAL: city gates opening)
Nadir Shah entered Delhi in March. For a brief moment, it looked like a negotiated occupation.

## [05:30] THE RUMOUR
(VISUAL: crowded bazaar at night)
Then a rumour spread that Nadir Shah had been killed. Some residents attacked Persian soldiers.

## [07:00] THE MASSACRE
(VISUAL: restraint: empty streets, smoke, no gore)
The response was a massacre that historians estimate killed tens of thousands of people in a single day.

## [09:30] THE TREASURE
(VISUAL: jewels, the Peacock Throne, the Koh-i-Noor)
He left with the Mughal treasury, the Peacock Throne and the Koh-i-Noor diamond.

## [12:00] AFTERMATH
The empire survived on paper, but its aura of invincibility was gone. Within decades, power in India would shift to new hands.') on conflict (script_id) do update set body = excluded.body;
